            <div xml:id="d486">

                <pb facs="0622" n="504" sameAs="frus:frus1928v02/pg504"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.512 Flour/1: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">MacMurray</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1928-06-26T16:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">June 26, 1928—4
                                p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 6:50 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>494. 1. Following from Shanghai: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“June 21, 12 a.m. [<hi rendition="#italic">noon.</hi>] Native press of
                            June 16th published regulations promulgated by the Ministry of Finance
                            to be immediately effective calling for special tax of 5 cents per sack
                            on Chinese and imported flour. Washington agreement authorizes eventual
                            levying of surtax on dutiable imports but since flour is now duty free
                            Legation’s instruction is requested in reference to request of American
                            flour importers that some action be taken by the American
                            Government.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>2. It is suggested Legation be authorized to instruct Cunningham to protest as a
                    matter of record.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">MacMurray</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
